# High-interaction shell decoy, built on Cowrie.
#
# WHY COWRIE
# ----------
# qeeqbox emulates protocols and logs credentials, but it does not give the
# attacker a real shell and it does not persist an uploaded file, so a payload
# dropped there vanishes. Cowrie is the industry-standard medium-interaction
# SSH honeypot whose core feature is exactly what we were missing: a full
# emulated shell where the attacker can wget/curl/scp/sftp a file, and Cowrie
# SAVES it — by sha256, tied to the command and the session.
#
# WHAT THE ATTACKER SEES
# ----------------------
# The SSH session is an EMULATED shell (Python/Twisted), not /bin/sh in this
# container. So the attacker never touches the real filesystem here — the
# "cowrie" paths in this image are invisible to them, and disguise is about the
# SSH banner and hostname, set in cowrie.cfg. The honest ceiling: Cowrie's
# emulated shell has known behavioural tells a skilled adversary can fingerprint
# (canned command output, fake-FS quirks). That is a property of every
# medium-interaction honeypot; the un-fingerprintable alternative is a real
# contained shell, which is real RCE.
#
# CONTAINMENT
# -----------
# Runs on the internal decoy_net with no route off the host (deploy_decoy.py).
# Consequence: Cowrie can only FETCH a wget/curl payload from hosts reachable on
# that network, not from the internet — a deliberate trade of wget-capture for
# no egress. Uploads (scp/sftp) and in-shell writes are captured regardless,
# and the attempted download URL is logged as intel even when the fetch cannot
# leave. Controlled egress for real payload fetch is a separate, deliberate step
# (route it through the RBI DLP proxy), never a side effect of running this.
FROM cowrie/cowrie:latest

# Our config overrides. userdb.txt (the accepted credentials) is generated from
# the canary vault and copied in at deploy time, so a token pasted into the
# dashboard becomes a working login here.
COPY docker/cowrie/cowrie.cfg /cowrie/cowrie-git/etc/cowrie.cfg
